<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temes County</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Temes County was a county of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>, with its seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temesvár</a>, now Timișoara. A medieval county until the Ottoman conquest of 1552, it was restored in 1779 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> returned the civil Banat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Its population was a mix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and Hungarians, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> its territory was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and the Kingdom of the Serbs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temes-county.jpg" alt="Temes County" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hogweard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temes_v%C3%A1rmegye_Magyarorsz%C3%A1gon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The county lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>, bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in the south and the Mureș in the north, and it shared borders with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and the Hungarian counties of Torontál, Arad and Krassó-Szörény. The Bega, Timiș, Bârzava and Caraș flowed through it, and in 1910 it covered 7,433 km2. Its seat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temesvár</a>, meaning Temes Castle, was also the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> between 1315 and 1323.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The county was formed in the 12th century, after Hungarian rule was established in the region, and was named after the Temes river; it is first mentioned in 1212. In 1070 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, devastated the area before King Géza I routed them, and King Ladislaus I repelled later Cuman and Byzantine raids. By the reign of King Béla III much of the land was settled by Slavic and Turkic groups. After the Ottoman conquest the territory belonged to the Temeşvar Eyalet, and in 1594-1595 the local Christians, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> uprising.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy took the region in the war of 1716-1718 and, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a>, made it the province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temeswar</a>. Between 1849 and 1860 the area belonged to the Voivodeship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and Temes Banat, an Austrian crownland; the county was recreated in January 1861 and enlarged in 1873 with parts of the former Banat Military Frontier, Fehértemplom among them. In the autumn of 1918 a short-lived Banat Republic was proclaimed at Temesvár. Hungary, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> all claimed the county until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> divided it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Romanian part of the old county now lies in Timiș County, except for a strip about 10 km wide along the Mureș, which is in Arad County. The south-western third, given to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, belongs to the Serbian autonomous region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, with the towns of Vršac, Bela Crkva and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kovin">Kovin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Temes County” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1778 Maria Theresa returned the civil Banat to Hungary as the counties of <b>Temes</b>, Torontál and Krassó, while its southern strip stayed in the Military Frontier.</blockquote><a href="/state/banat-of-temeswar">Banat of Temeswar</a></li></ul></section>
